The goal of this experiment is to understand how the scientific method works. To do this you will make observations, look for patterns and come up with a hypothesis to \textit{identify an unknown compound}. You will do this by comparing the reaction of the unknown compound with a set of known--reference--compounds, also called blanks.
In this experiment, you will obtain some blank chemicals and an unknown. Your unknown is the same as one of the blanks. You will test each of these chemicals with a set of reagents. You have to observe the result of the test and record any change, such as \textit{gas evolution}, the formation of a \textit{precipitate}, a \textit{change of color}, or maybe \textit{no reaction}. Simultaneously, you will run similar tests for the unknown sample that is identical to one of the blanks. You will determine the unknown's identity by matching its characteristic reactions with one of the blanks.
